INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "Mandiant 3CX Software Supply Chain Compromise investigation", "source_url": "https://cloud.google.com/blog/topics/threat-intelligence/3cx-software-supply-chain-compromise", "text": "Mandiant reports the initial 3CX network compromise vector was malicious software downloaded from Trading Technologies' website: a tampered X_TRADER installer, an earlier software supply-chain compromise. The attacker used X_TRADER on a 3CX employee machine, then moved laterally in 3CX, harvested credentials, and compromised both Windows and macOS build environments. Windows build environment: TAXHAUL launcher and COLDCAT downloader persisted via IKEEXT DLL search-order hijacking with LocalSystem privileges. macOS build server: POOLRAT backdoor persisted with Launch Daemons; prior reporting called it SIMPLESEA, but Mandiant identified it as POOLRAT. The attacker then trojanized 3CX DesktopApp builds. Affected 3CX DesktopApp 18.12.416 and earlier ran SUDDENICON, which obtained C2 URLs from encrypted GitHub-hosted icons and downloaded ICONICSTEALER browser-information dataminer. Mandiant tracks the activity as UNC4736, a suspected North Korean nexus cluster. Mandiant assesses with moderate confidence UNC4736 is related to financially motivated North Korean AppleJeus activity; evidence includes overlap with Trading Technologies compromise and POOLRAT associated with CoinGoTrade/JMT Trading AppleJeus activity. Mandiant notes weak infrastructure overlap with suspected APT43 clusters UNC3782 and UNC4469. The X_TRADER and 3CX samples shared RC4 key, SIGFLIP, DAVESHELL, hardcoded cookie variable, and AES-256-GCM encryption. Mandiant says this was a cascading supply-chain compromise."}
